1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:03,880
Hi! Brandon here. It is National Novel Writing

2
00:00:03,880 --> 00:00:07,440
Month. This is a challenge that happens every

3
00:00:07,440 --> 00:00:10,800
year where writers are challenged to write a

4
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:14,160
novel in one month, loosely defining a novel

5
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:17,160
as 50,000 words. That definition is kind of vague.

6
00:00:17,160 --> 00:00:20,160
Most of my novels are quite a bit longer. But

7
00:00:20,160 --> 00:00:23,800
50,000 words is a doable but difficult challenge

8
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:27,440
for one month of writing. The goal of this

9
00:00:27,440 --> 00:00:30,760
challenge is to get yourself out of a rut,

10
00:00:30,760 --> 00:00:34,080
to force yourself to turn off your internal editor,

11
00:00:34,080 --> 00:00:36,840
and to just write. It's a really great exercise.

12
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:39,600
I did it numerous times before I became a published

13
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:42,040
writer. In fact, The Way of Kings, which my

14
00:00:42,040 --> 00:00:44,480
new book in the series is coming out this month,

15
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:48,320
was originally written as part of a National

16
00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:50,960
Novel Writing Month challenge. So I think this

17
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:54,160
is a very worthy challenge. Like all things

18
00:00:54,160 --> 00:00:57,360
in writing, it might not work for you.

19
00:00:57,360 --> 00:00:57,390
I'm not going to be doing this. I'm going to be doing this

20
00:00:57,390 --> 00:00:57,420
for a long time. I'm going to be doing this for a long time.

21
00:00:57,420 --> 00:01:01,570
You may try it and find out it just,

22
00:01:01,570 --> 00:01:05,720
your personal writing psychology, just does not mesh really well

23
00:01:05,720 --> 00:01:08,180
with this type of challenge. You might find that

24
00:01:08,180 --> 00:01:10,640
the writing you write, trying to follow a challenge

25
00:01:10,640 --> 00:01:13,500
like this, just is not as good as if you

26
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:16,360
didn't. In that case, feel free to abandon it. However,

27
00:01:16,360 --> 00:01:18,860
for those of you who want to try this and

28
00:01:18,860 --> 00:01:21,360
have never done it before, I'm going to give you five

29
00:01:21,360 --> 00:01:24,350
hacks, five ways that you can kind of

30
00:01:24,350 --> 00:01:27,340
start writing a novel, perhaps without very much preparation,

31
00:01:27,340 --> 00:01:30,930
and kind of force yourself to try this challenge. So

32
00:01:30,930 --> 00:01:34,520
first one is something that a lot of us authors

33
00:01:34,520 --> 00:01:37,220
do, which is called borrowing your structure.

34
00:01:37,220 --> 00:01:39,920
Now, it doesn't happen with every novel.

35
00:01:40,760 --> 00:01:43,720
In fact, it doesn't happen with every writer. But a

36
00:01:43,720 --> 00:01:46,680
lot of times, books that we write come from us

37
00:01:46,680 --> 00:01:49,960
watching a movie, reading a story, hearing about

38
00:01:49,960 --> 00:01:53,240
an event, and really liking that genre or that

39
00:01:53,240 --> 00:01:55,250
type of story, and saying, man, could I write something like that?

40
00:01:55,250 --> 00:01:57,260
Could I write something like that? Could I write something like that?

41
00:01:57,260 --> 00:02:00,940
Then as a writer, we dig into that subgenre, say

42
00:02:00,940 --> 00:02:04,620
the heist novel, and say, what are some of the

43
00:02:04,620 --> 00:02:07,180
aspects of this type of story that make them

44
00:02:07,180 --> 00:02:09,740
really interesting? Why do I like a heist novel?

45
00:02:09,740 --> 00:02:12,540
What about it is great? You know what? I

46
00:02:12,540 --> 00:02:15,340
really like that heists involve a crew of different

47
00:02:15,340 --> 00:02:18,740
people who all have a different speciality

48
00:02:18,740 --> 00:02:22,140
who come together and apply their specialized talents

49
00:02:22,140 --> 00:02:24,540
to achieving this really difficult goal.

50
00:02:24,540 --> 00:02:26,940
And suddenly you have a story that,

51
00:02:27,260 --> 00:02:29,380
as a writer, has a structure to it. You realize,

52
00:02:29,380 --> 00:02:31,500
hey, we have a scene where you outline the problem.

53
00:02:31,500 --> 00:02:33,940
We have a scene with each of

54
00:02:33,940 --> 00:02:36,380
the characters being recruited, whether it's Ocean's Eleven,

55
00:02:36,380 --> 00:02:40,180
or whether it is Armageddon, or whether

56
00:02:40,180 --> 00:02:43,980
it is Inception. They all share some hallmark

57
00:02:43,980 --> 00:02:46,820
pieces of their structure. You are followed

58
00:02:46,820 --> 00:02:49,660
by some extra training or gathering of equipment

59
00:02:49,660 --> 00:02:51,820
where they plan on how they're going to do

60
00:02:51,820 --> 00:02:53,980
this. And then you have an ending where you bring

61
00:02:53,980 --> 00:02:55,620
together everyone's specialized talents

62
00:02:55,620 --> 00:02:57,260
to overcome the problem.

63
00:02:57,260 --> 00:02:58,340
And then you have a movie where you

64
00:02:58,340 --> 00:02:59,420
have a story that you're going to write about.

65
00:02:59,420 --> 00:03:00,460
And then you have a movie where you

66
00:03:00,460 --> 00:03:01,500
have a story that you're going to write about.

67
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:02,780
And then you have a movie where you

68
00:03:02,780 --> 00:03:04,060
have a story that you're going to write about.

69
00:03:04,060 --> 00:03:07,060
One way to get yourself into writing and kind

70
00:03:07,060 --> 00:03:10,060
of maybe have, so to speak, some training wheels on,

71
00:03:10,060 --> 00:03:12,660
is to go watch that movie and say, all

72
00:03:12,660 --> 00:03:15,260
right, can I boil this down to its fundamental structure?

73
00:03:15,260 --> 00:03:18,220
Then rebuild that structure with a new set

74
00:03:18,220 --> 00:03:21,180
of characters and a new problem to solve,

75
00:03:22,060 --> 00:03:24,620
and a new character arc that is my own.

76
00:03:24,620 --> 00:03:27,180
A lot of times this works really well if you

77
00:03:27,260 --> 00:03:31,579
transpose the genre. If you, say, really love

78
00:03:31,579 --> 00:03:35,899
the stories that are told in Regency romances

79
00:03:35,899 --> 00:03:38,899
done by Jane Austen, and you say, what if I

80
00:03:38,899 --> 00:03:41,899
took this same structure and I made it a Western

81
00:03:41,899 --> 00:03:44,419
instead? These sorts of transpositions can also help

82
00:03:44,419 --> 00:03:46,940
you add a little more flair, a little bit

83
00:03:46,940 --> 00:03:48,899
more of your own style, but you still, like

84
00:03:48,899 --> 00:03:50,859
I said, have those training wheels. It doesn't have

85
00:03:50,859 --> 00:03:52,979
to be training wheels, though. A lot of professional

86
00:03:52,979 --> 00:03:55,100
writers will do this same sort of thing when

87
00:03:55,100 --> 00:03:56,059
coming up with a story. They'll look to the

88
00:03:56,059 --> 00:03:57,019
success of the story, and they'll look to the

89
00:03:57,019 --> 00:03:57,099
success of the story. They'll look to the success

90
00:03:57,099 --> 00:03:57,180
of the story. They'll look to the success of the

91
00:03:57,180 --> 00:03:57,200
story. They'll look to the success of the story.

92
00:03:57,200 --> 00:03:57,220
They'll look to the success of the story. They'll look

93
00:03:57,220 --> 00:03:57,300
to the success of the story. They'll look to

94
00:03:57,300 --> 00:03:57,380
the success of the story. They'll look to the successful

95
00:03:57,380 --> 00:03:59,580
stories told of this trope in

96
00:03:59,580 --> 00:04:01,780
the past and borrow that structure.

97
00:04:01,780 --> 00:04:03,860
Do be aware that you shouldn't be enslaved by

98
00:04:03,860 --> 00:04:05,940
that structure. You should be able to adapt it

99
00:04:05,940 --> 00:04:08,020
to your story. But it really can

100
00:04:08,020 --> 00:04:10,100
help, particularly if you're trying to write

101
00:04:10,100 --> 00:04:12,700
something in one month, to have

102
00:04:12,700 --> 00:04:15,300
a structure, an outline already provided.

103
00:04:16,420 --> 00:04:19,300
Number two cool way to start writing a book if

104
00:04:19,300 --> 00:04:22,180
you've never tried it is to begin with a monologue.

105
00:04:22,180 --> 00:04:24,660
Even if your story isn't going to be

106
00:04:24,660 --> 00:04:27,140
first-person perspective, meaning it's not going to be told,

107
00:04:27,140 --> 00:04:30,619
from one character's viewpoint, telling the story as

108
00:04:30,619 --> 00:04:34,099
they experienced it. You can still really get

109
00:04:34,099 --> 00:04:36,899
into a character by basically interviewing them, or

110
00:04:36,899 --> 00:04:39,700
having them tell you about a really important

111
00:04:39,700 --> 00:04:41,860
time in their life, and you write it out

112
00:04:41,860 --> 00:04:44,020
as if they were sitting there and telling you about

113
00:04:44,020 --> 00:04:46,420
their story. A good friend of mine, Dan

114
00:04:46,420 --> 00:04:48,820
Wells, several of his books began with him saying,

115
00:04:48,820 --> 00:04:50,780
all right, if this character were going to

116
00:04:50,780 --> 00:04:52,740
explain their life to me in five pages,

117
00:04:52,740 --> 00:04:56,460
what would it be? And he wrote those five

118
00:04:56,460 --> 00:05:00,180
pages, and then that was the character kind of explaining

119
00:05:00,180 --> 00:05:02,660
themselves to Dan. Really, it was Dan

120
00:05:02,660 --> 00:05:05,140
figuring out interesting things about this character.

121
00:05:05,140 --> 00:05:07,660
Now, this probably won't end up in your final book.

122
00:05:07,660 --> 00:05:10,180
But one of the cool things is, is if you

123
00:05:10,180 --> 00:05:12,340
design it the right way, this can become,

124
00:05:12,340 --> 00:05:14,500
pieces of this can be the little blurbs,

125
00:05:14,500 --> 00:05:16,460
we call them epigraphs, at the start

126
00:05:16,460 --> 00:05:18,420
of chapters. Could be excerpts from the character's

127
00:05:18,420 --> 00:05:20,540
journal or diary or something like that. Maybe

128
00:05:20,540 --> 00:05:22,660
you'll just love this format and write what we

129
00:05:22,660 --> 00:05:22,700
want to write. But if you're going to write a

130
00:05:22,700 --> 00:05:22,740
book, you're going to want to write a book that's

131
00:05:22,740 --> 00:05:25,260
called an epistolary novel, which is a novel

132
00:05:25,260 --> 00:05:27,780
written entirely in journal forms or in people's

133
00:05:27,780 --> 00:05:30,420
writing. It's kind of the found footage version of

134
00:05:30,420 --> 00:05:33,060
novels. So give that a try. Just have the

135
00:05:33,060 --> 00:05:34,860
character write as if you

136
00:05:34,860 --> 00:05:36,660
were them explaining about their life.

137
00:05:37,460 --> 00:05:41,180
Number three, one way to really dig into

138
00:05:41,180 --> 00:05:44,900
a character is to ask yourself, what does my

139
00:05:44,900 --> 00:05:48,780
character want? What do they need? How are those

140
00:05:48,780 --> 00:05:52,660
two things different? And why can't they have either

141
00:05:52,660 --> 00:05:56,140
one? And if you ask yourself these things, it can

142
00:05:56,140 --> 00:05:59,620
help you to generate and construct a plot even on

143
00:05:59,620 --> 00:06:01,940
the fly. You start writing your characters.

144
00:06:01,940 --> 00:06:04,260
Just sit down, start writing their everyday life,

145
00:06:04,260 --> 00:06:06,780
figure out what it is that they really want.

146
00:06:06,780 --> 00:06:09,300
Ask yourself how that's different from what they need.

147
00:06:09,300 --> 00:06:11,060
Just do it as you're exploring this

148
00:06:11,060 --> 00:06:12,820
character's life in the first few chapters.

149
00:06:12,820 --> 00:06:15,540
Then start to construct the obstacles that keep

150
00:06:15,540 --> 00:06:18,260
them from having these things that they want.

151
00:06:18,260 --> 00:06:20,420
This is just a really good way to

152
00:06:20,420 --> 00:06:22,580
start making sure that your story is character-centered.

153
00:06:22,580 --> 00:06:25,660
If your story is turning around this character's

154
00:06:25,660 --> 00:06:28,740
needs and wants, then you will naturally be

155
00:06:28,740 --> 00:06:31,940
including the character in a lot of the story

156
00:06:31,940 --> 00:06:35,140
decisions. One way you can go wrong with a book

157
00:06:35,140 --> 00:06:37,260
is by making the character not want or need

158
00:06:37,260 --> 00:06:39,380
anything. And a lot of times what will happen

159
00:06:39,380 --> 00:06:41,899
is that character becomes an external observer

160
00:06:41,899 --> 00:06:44,419
of some really interesting story happening with some

161
00:06:44,419 --> 00:06:47,259
other character. And as a writing professor, I see

162
00:06:47,259 --> 00:06:50,099
this quite a bit from new writers, where they've

163
00:06:50,099 --> 00:06:52,500
picked the wrong viewpoint. Someone really interesting is

164
00:06:52,500 --> 00:06:52,540
going to be the one who's going to be the one

165
00:06:52,540 --> 00:06:52,580
who's going to be the one who's going to be the

166
00:06:52,580 --> 00:06:54,140
one who's doing something. And this character

167
00:06:54,140 --> 00:06:55,700
that they thought would be their main character

168
00:06:55,700 --> 00:06:58,619
is just observing and commenting on it. You want

169
00:06:58,619 --> 00:07:01,539
to avoid that. You want the story to be personal

170
00:07:01,539 --> 00:07:04,019
to your viewpoint character. You always want to

171
00:07:04,019 --> 00:07:06,500
be picking the viewpoint character who is either

172
00:07:06,500 --> 00:07:10,780
changing the most, having the most conflict in

173
00:07:10,780 --> 00:07:15,060
their life, or who is actively working on getting

174
00:07:15,060 --> 00:07:17,460
what they want the most. Those are good ways

175
00:07:17,460 --> 00:07:19,860
to choose who the protagonist of your story might be.

176
00:07:20,500 --> 00:07:22,500
Now, number four is

177
00:07:22,500 --> 00:07:24,580
going to go hand in hand with this.

178
00:07:24,580 --> 00:07:26,660
Let's say you're working on this, but you're like,

179
00:07:26,660 --> 00:07:28,620
Brandon, I still don't have a structure for

180
00:07:28,620 --> 00:07:30,580
my story. I know what my character can't have.

181
00:07:30,580 --> 00:07:33,180
I know why they want it so badly. And

182
00:07:33,180 --> 00:07:35,780
I know the difference between their wants and their need.

183
00:07:36,580 --> 00:07:38,740
I know what they think they want

184
00:07:38,740 --> 00:07:40,900
and what will actually make them happy,

185
00:07:40,900 --> 00:07:43,340
but I don't have a structure for my

186
00:07:43,340 --> 00:07:45,780
story. In this case, number four tip is,

187
00:07:45,780 --> 00:07:49,100
choose your type of progress. I have an entire lecture

188
00:07:49,100 --> 00:07:52,420
on this, but if I boil it down, stories are

189
00:07:52,420 --> 00:07:55,740
built from three ideas. They're a promise,

190
00:07:55,740 --> 00:07:59,060
progress toward that promise, and a payoff on

191
00:07:59,060 --> 00:08:00,940
that promise. And the progress part is the

192
00:08:00,940 --> 00:08:02,820
important part. That's what the bulk of your

193
00:08:02,820 --> 00:08:06,020
story is. We get pulled through stories. Things

194
00:08:06,020 --> 00:08:09,220
become page turners because we can watch progress

195
00:08:09,220 --> 00:08:11,860
toward a goal. And books naturally have this just in

196
00:08:11,860 --> 00:08:14,500
the shape of the book. You get to watch. You're

197
00:08:14,500 --> 00:08:16,820
like, this book is 500 pages long. As I

198
00:08:16,820 --> 00:08:19,140
read, I'm getting closer to that ending. But you want

199
00:08:19,140 --> 00:08:20,700
to reinforce this with some sort of plot that

200
00:08:20,700 --> 00:08:22,260
you are showing progress toward. You're going to want

201
00:08:22,260 --> 00:08:22,330
to reinforce this with some sort of plot that

202
00:08:22,330 --> 00:08:22,400
you are showing progress toward. You're going to want

203
00:08:22,400 --> 00:08:22,800
to reinforce this with some sort of

204
00:08:22,800 --> 00:08:23,200
plot that you are showing progress toward.

205
00:08:23,200 --> 00:08:25,240
Now, in a lot of stories, this

206
00:08:25,240 --> 00:08:27,280
might be, for instance, a travelogue. You say,

207
00:08:27,280 --> 00:08:30,440
we are starting in the Shire, and we are going

208
00:08:30,440 --> 00:08:33,600
to end at Mount Doom, where the ring was forged.

209
00:08:33,600 --> 00:08:36,159
And we can watch on the map as the

210
00:08:36,159 --> 00:08:38,720
characters get closer and closer and closer. We can see

211
00:08:38,720 --> 00:08:40,639
their progress. We can see where they

212
00:08:40,639 --> 00:08:42,559
get diverted and can't go the direction where

213
00:08:42,559 --> 00:08:44,959
they thought they were going to go, because

214
00:08:44,959 --> 00:08:47,360
it's difficult. You want always in your story

215
00:08:47,360 --> 00:08:49,800
to be moving in a direction,

216
00:08:49,800 --> 00:08:52,240
mostly forward, sometimes backward. And this

217
00:08:52,240 --> 00:08:55,160
progress does not have to be travelogue.

218
00:08:55,160 --> 00:08:58,080
In a mystery, usually the progress is clues.

219
00:08:58,080 --> 00:09:00,680
It's an information progress, meaning you have a

220
00:09:00,680 --> 00:09:03,280
mystery. You don't know who killed this person.

221
00:09:03,280 --> 00:09:05,560
Your characters are going to get clues, information that

222
00:09:05,560 --> 00:09:07,840
build upon one another. And some of them are

223
00:09:07,840 --> 00:09:09,640
false clues. Some of them are steps backward.

224
00:09:09,640 --> 00:09:11,440
Some of them lead you in the wrong direction.

225
00:09:11,440 --> 00:09:13,800
But you can get a sense as a reader that

226
00:09:13,800 --> 00:09:16,160
more clues are gathering, and the image of who did

227
00:09:16,160 --> 00:09:19,120
this is becoming more and more and more clear.

228
00:09:19,120 --> 00:09:22,080
So decide your type of progress, and make sure

229
00:09:22,080 --> 00:09:24,800
that you are signposting that progress is being

230
00:09:24,800 --> 00:09:27,520
made. Most of the time, when readers complain

231
00:09:27,520 --> 00:09:30,360
about a story not moving quickly enough, not

232
00:09:30,360 --> 00:09:33,200
having good pacing, or not being a page turner,

233
00:09:33,200 --> 00:09:35,840
it's because the author is not signposting the

234
00:09:35,840 --> 00:09:38,480
type of progress that the story is supposed to

235
00:09:38,480 --> 00:09:40,760
be making. You can misalign this sometimes. You

236
00:09:40,760 --> 00:09:43,040
can say you're writing a romance when really

237
00:09:43,040 --> 00:09:45,040
you're writing an adventure story, and so

238
00:09:45,040 --> 00:09:47,040
you signpost the romance, but the reader's feeling

239
00:09:47,040 --> 00:09:49,360
like we aren't making any progress because

240
00:09:49,360 --> 00:09:51,680
you gave a bigger signpost about the adventure,

241
00:09:51,680 --> 00:09:51,880
and there's no progress. But you give a

242
00:09:51,880 --> 00:09:52,080
bigger signpost about the adventure, and there's a bigger

243
00:09:52,080 --> 00:09:54,520
reader thinks this is what the story's about. It is

244
00:09:54,520 --> 00:09:56,960
possible to do both hand in hand, and a lot of

245
00:09:56,960 --> 00:09:59,960
stories do this. But my tip to you is decide

246
00:09:59,960 --> 00:10:02,960
what type of progress are you going to expect the

247
00:10:02,960 --> 00:10:05,960
reader to feel moving through the story, and make sure

248
00:10:05,960 --> 00:10:08,960
you split that up, divide it up, give us a

249
00:10:08,960 --> 00:10:12,200
trail of breadcrumbs along the way, indicating

250
00:10:12,200 --> 00:10:15,440
that we're making that progress. The last piece

251
00:10:15,440 --> 00:10:19,200
of advice I would give you to try to write a

252
00:10:19,200 --> 00:10:22,960
book in a month is to prime your mind. Before I write,

253
00:10:22,960 --> 00:10:26,040
I really like to do something else for a

254
